A. Who takes which term (illustrative amplitudes from datum-02's default tube; rms of a sinusoid is amplitude / sqrt 2; peak is the in-phase worst case)

   nothing (as hand-aimed, uncompensated)                     radial rms 0.223 peak<= 0.38   vertical rms 0.185 peak<= 0.33   both rms 0.290   (the scene says 0.29)
   static trim: one number per axis per tube (hand, shim, or a stage set once) radial rms 0.098 peak<= 0.18   vertical rms 0.108 peak<= 0.18   both rms 0.146
     + radial 1x taken out at its source (nest screws, travel-06: 90 percent) radial rms 0.037 peak<= 0.06   vertical rms 0.108 peak<= 0.18   both rms 0.114
     + vertical 1x by a Z follow under the work (95 percent)  radial rms 0.037 peak<= 0.06   vertical rms 0.022 peak<= 0.04   both rms 0.043
     + 2x terms replayed too (all terms, ideal)               radial rms 0.000 peak<= 0.00   vertical rms 0.000 peak<= 0.00   both rms 0.000   (fit noise leaves about 0.022 in the scene)

   So: the constant terms are two thirds of the uncompensated rms (0.29 -> 0.15 by a static trim). Against a window of +-0.30 mm the static trim alone leaves
   the dot inside the window everywhere (peaks 0.18 radial, 0.18 vertical); a replay stage earns its place only when the window is below about 0.2 mm, and
   then it is the vertical axis that is needed first (the 1x face wobble cannot be taken out by any screw in the nest).

B. The hold between the dry turn and the weld turn. The replay applies the dry-turn constant term; anything that moves the gun relative to the room between
   the two turns adds a constant the fit never saw. Error = change of force x compliance of the chain from gun to room. Change of force: [unknown]
   (wire feeder starting and its conduit, gas hose, fibre emitting, hands off). Compliance per newton from calc/06:
   holder                                                                            0.3 N     1 N       3 N   (mm of constant error)
   20 mm steel rod, 300 mm, bolted                                                      0.002    0.006    0.017
   12 mm steel rod, 300 mm, bolted                                                      0.013    0.044    0.133
   12 mm aluminium rod, 300 mm                                                          0.038    0.128    0.384
   (A friction-locked joint is not a spring: calc/06 B gives 0.087 mm for 0.02 degree of creep at a 250 mm lever, whatever the force.)
   Compare with the fit residual, 0.022 rms: a 1 N change against a 12 mm steel rod is twice that and the same sign every lap.
   A stage carried in the gun's shell is in series with the holder: add its stiffness. A 20 N/mm stage gives 0.05 mm per newton on top of the holder.

   Requirement to keep the parity error under 0.02 mm at a 1 N change: chain compliance under 20 um/N, i.e. stiffer than 50 N/mm gun-to-room, stage included.

C. Backlash on a horizontal axis. The replay reverses at the peaks of a sinusoid. With a backlash b in the stage and no preload (a horizontal axis has no gravity),
   the load sits on one flank and lags the command by up to b at each reversal: the swing is b peak to peak, so b/2 either side once the mean is trimmed:
   backlash 0.02 mm -> residual amplitude 0.010 mm, rms 0.010 mm (radial 1x + 2x terms replayed)
   backlash 0.05 mm -> residual amplitude 0.025 mm, rms 0.024 mm (radial 1x + 2x terms replayed)
   backlash 0.10 mm -> residual amplitude 0.050 mm, rms 0.046 mm (radial 1x + 2x terms replayed)
   A vertical axis is loaded one way by the gun's (or the rotator's) weight and does not see this; a horizontal one wants a light spring preload or a flexure.
